\label{inY}
If $\mathscr{B}\subset{\mathbb{N}}$ is taut then $\eta\in Y$.
\begin{Remark}
It is not so hard to see that $Y$ may not be closed, so even if $\eta\in Y$ then $X_\eta\subseteq Y$ is not automatic. Indeed, let $\mathscr{B}$ be the set of squares of all primes. Then $X_\eta$ is hereditary and it is equal to the set of admissible sequences. Then $\eta\in Y$, so for any  $b\in\mathscr{B}$, there exists $m$ such that that $|{\rm supp }(\eta) \cap[-m,m] \bmod b|=b-1$. Moreover, each block that appears on $\eta$ is of positive Mirsky measure, so, in particular, it appears on $\eta$ infinitely often. This implies that $|{\rm supp }(\eta \cdot\mathbbm{1}_{{\mathbb{Z}}\setminus [-n,n]}) \bmod b|=b-1$ for any $n\geq 1$. In other words, $\eta \cdot\mathbbm{1}_{{\mathbb{Z}}\setminus [-n,n]}\in Y$. Clearly, $\eta \cdot\mathbbm{1}_{{\mathbb{Z}}\setminus [-n,n]}$ converges to the all-zero sequence as $n$ tends to infinity, which means that $Y$ is not closed.

In fact, it follows from Theorem~\ref{onedim} that $Y_\eta:=Y\cap X_\eta$ is closed and non-empty precisely if $\eta$ is Toeplitz. Indeed, if $Y_\eta$ is closed and non-empty then necessarily $\eta\in Y_\eta$, which yields $Y_\eta=X_\eta$ and this is equivalent to $X_\eta\subset Y$, so, by Theorem~\ref{onedim}, $\eta$ is Toeplitz. To obtain the implication in the other direction, notice that if $\eta$ is Toeplitz then $X_\eta=Y_\eta$ and it is immediate that $Y_\eta$ is closed and non-empty.
\end{Remark}

